  "The FamilySearch Wiki page 'Pennsylvania Coal Region Records' names a Lackawanna Valley Anthracite Index, 1855-1898 (~41,000 colliery workers, from payroll books surviving a 1903 records consolidation) covering the lower anthracite field — this is centered on Lackawanna County, not Schuylkill, so confirm coverage before relying on it for a Schuylkill-based miner.",
  "Catholic parish jurisdiction for the five coal counties split in 1868: before 1868 all five (Schuylkill, Luzerne, Lackawanna, Northumberland, Carbon) were under the Diocese of Philadelphia; from 1868 Luzerne and Lackawanna moved to the new Diocese of Scranton and Northumberland to the Diocese of Harrisburg, while Schuylkill and Carbon remained with Philadelphia until the Diocese of Allentown was created in 1961 — so a Schuylkill County Catholic baptism/marriage/burial register in this 1860s-1890s window is held by the Archdiocese of Philadelphia, not Scranton.",
  "Pennsylvania state tax assessors enumerated coal-region residents triennially from 1853; the assessment ledgers can substitute for missing vital/census data and sometimes record nativity.",
